# C / C++ coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern C++
(17/20/23) and modern C idiom. Applies to both `.c`/`.h` and
`.cpp`/`.cc`/`.cxx`/`.hpp`/`.hxx` files — everything not C/C++-specific
still comes from the common rules this file `extends`.

## Naming and structure

- `snake_case` for functions, variables, and namespaces; `PascalCase` for
  types (classes, structs, enums); `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for macros and
  compile-time constants — match whichever convention the project already
  uses over this default when one is established.
- Keep a header self-contained: `#pragma once` (or a project-consistent
  include guard) at the top, and every symbol the header uses actually
  `#include`d in it — do not rely on an include some other header happens
  to pull in transitively.
- One translation unit's private helpers go in an anonymous namespace
  (C++) or are declared `static` (C) — do not give internal linkage away
  by leaving a file-local helper externally visible.
- An identifier's name shrinks with its scope: a loop index is `i`, a
  header-exposed public function gets a full descriptive name.

## Modern C++ idiom (17/20/23)

- Own a heap allocation with `std::unique_ptr` by default; reach for
  `std::shared_ptr` only when the ownership is genuinely shared (more than
  one owner can outlive any single other owner) — a `shared_ptr` used for
  single ownership just adds atomic refcount overhead and obscures who
  actually owns the object.
- Prefer `std::make_unique`/`std::make_shared` over a bare `new` passed to
  a smart pointer constructor — it avoids a leak on an exception thrown
  between the `new` and the constructor call, and avoids a second
  allocation for `shared_ptr`'s control block.
- Use `std::span` (C++20) for a non-owning view over a contiguous range
  instead of a raw `(pointer, length)` pair — it carries bounds
  information the raw pair does not, without owning or copying the data.
- Move, don't copy, when a value's ownership is transferring: accept
  by-value and `std::move` into place, or take an rvalue-reference
  overload, rather than accepting `const&` and copying when the caller's
  object was about to be discarded anyway.
- Prefer a range-`for` or a `<ranges>` (C++20) pipeline over a manual
  index loop or raw iterator pair when the loop is a plain traversal —
  keep the manual iterator form for cases that need the iterator itself
  (insertion position, mutation while iterating with an explicit
  erase-returns-next-iterator pattern).
- `constexpr`/`consteval` any function or value that can be computed at
  compile time; `const` (or nothing, per project convention) otherwise —
  do not default to `#define` for a typed constant.

## Modern C safety practices

- Prefer a fixed-size or length-tracked buffer API (`snprintf`, not
  `sprintf`; `strncpy`/explicit length checks, not `strcpy`/`strcat`;
  `fgets`, never `gets`) — every string/buffer write states or checks its
  destination's capacity.
- Check every `malloc`/`calloc`/`realloc` return for `NULL` before
  dereferencing it; on `realloc` failure, keep the original pointer (do
  not overwrite it with the `NULL` return and leak the original block).
- Prefer C99 designated initializers and stack-based fixed arrays with an
  explicit, checked bound over a raw pointer with an implicit length
  carried only in a comment.
- Const-qualify a pointer parameter the function does not mutate through
  (`const char *`, not `char *`) — it documents the contract and lets the
  compiler catch an accidental write.

## Formatting

- Format with the project's configured `clang-format`/`.clang-format` if
  present; do not hand-format around a formatter that is already
  configured.
- Braces and brace-placement follow the project's existing style over any
  default preference in this file — match neighboring code.
